# End-to-end (E2E) tests

Top of the pyramid: **few**, high-fidelity checks of critical journeys. Expensive and flake-prone — use sparingly (Home Office / GitLab guidance).

## What belongs here

- Real process or **container** listening on a port
- Critical path only: health + one successful estimate **or** CI Docker smoke
- Optional: OpenAPI `/docs` reachable

## Rules

- Cover **riskiest user/API journeys**, not every endpoint permutation
- Prefer stable assertions (status codes, JSON keys) over brittle full-body matches
- Isolate secrets: use test keys / mocks unless an explicit paid “live LLM” job exists
- Run in CI less often or as a dedicated job if slow; never block on dozens of e2e
- Clean up containers/processes; fail with logs on timeout

## Example targets in this repo

- Build image → run container → `GET /health` (already in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`)
- Optional: `POST /api/v1/estimate` against container with mocked provider sidecar or stubbed env — only if valuable beyond integration

## Do not

- Replace unit/integration coverage with e2e
- Call paid LLMs on every PR by default
- Large UI/browser suites unless a frontend exists
- Flaky sleeps without bounded retries and clear failure logs

## Fidelity vs cost (SMURF)

E2E maximizes **fidelity**; unit maximizes **speed/reliability**. Keep e2e thin so the suite stays maintainable.
